course: dictionary
lesson: 10721
title: Unwilling and its word family
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Unwilling is made of two parts. Un- means not, and willing means ready and wanting to do something. So unwilling means not wanting to do something. Look at the root word willing and the meaning often follows.
explain: «Mum was unwilling to let me stay up late.» Mum did not want to, so she said no. An unwilling helper does a job only because they have to, not because they want to. Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: "Mum was not wanting to let me stay up late" still makes sense.
explain: Word families share a root. Unwilling is an adjective, unwillingly is an adverb that tells how something is done, and unwillingness is a noun for the feeling itself. «He tidied his room unwillingly.» «Her unwillingness showed in her slow steps.»
fact: Unwilling means not wanting to do something, so you do it only because you have to.
fact: Unwilling to do something means you refuse or are not ready to do it.
fact: «Clingy» means anxious and unwilling to leave the parent.
fact: «Budget-conscious» means poor or unwilling to spend.
fact: Word family: unwilling (adjective), unwillingly (adverb), unwillingness (noun).
exercise: «The unwilling helper sighed and picked up the broom.» What does unwilling mean? | Happy and eager to help | Very tired from running | *Not wanting to do it, but doing it because you have to | Lost and confused
exercise: «She was unwilling to pay more than 20 dollars.» What does unwilling to pay mean here? | Able to pay any amount | Eager to spend every cent | *Refusing or not ready to pay more than 20 dollars | Sure she had lost her purse
exercise: Ben tidied his room unwillingly. Which word tells you how he tidied? | tidied | room | *unwillingly | Ben
test: «Clingy» means anxious and unwilling to leave the parent. In that sentence, what does unwilling mean? | Very hungry | Unable to speak | *Not wanting to leave | Eager to go
test: Which word names the feeling of being unwilling? | unwilling | willingly | *unwillingness | unwillingly
exam: Sam was an ___ volunteer, but he helped anyway. Which word fits the gap? | eager | cheerful | *unwilling | busy
